Node Specifications
3BM — Fast Memory Tagger
Model: Hermes-3-Llama-3.2-3B Q8_0
Role: Session memory and intent classification
Status: Verified from reconstructed source code
What it does:
Reads the user message and produces a small JSON object of tags, goals, and constraints. It does not reason. It does not recall prior sessions. It reads the current message and returns structured metadata that all downstream nodes use to make routing and reasoning decisions.
Inference parameters (verified):
Max tokens: 256
Temperature: 0.4 (low — consistent, not creative)
Context window: 2048 tokens
Top-p: 0.95
Input: Raw user message text
Output: JSON object — tags, goals, constraints
Activates: Always. Every request passes through 3BM first.
Logging: Every run logged to ~/justicar/state/logs/memory/ with timestamp
Example output structure:
Json
8BM — General Recall
Model: 8B class (specific model reconstructed from architecture, not verified code)
Role: Cross-session context retrieval
Status: Behavioral description — architect's direct experience
What it does:
Retrieves relevant context from prior sessions when the current request requires history beyond the active conversation. Does not activate for every request — only when 3BM tags indicate cross-session context would improve the response.
Activation condition: Request requires context beyond current session. For users with significant conversation history, this activates frequently. For new users with no history, it may not activate at all.
Input: User message, 3BM tags JSON
Output: Bundle markdown — relevant prior context assembled for downstream nodes
Activates: Conditionally, based on planner decision and 3BM tag signals
14BM — Architectural Memory
Model: 14B class
Role: Long-term architectural and constitutional memory
Status: Behavioral description — architect's direct experience
What it does:
Holds the deep knowledge graph, constitutional framework, and long-term architectural context of the whole system. Slow to update — this is the persistent memory of who the system is and what it knows at the deepest level. Not activated for routine requests.
Input: Complex cross-session context requiring deep architectural grounding
Output: Deep context bundle for high-stakes reasoning chains
3BR — Quick Reasoning
Model: 3B class
Role: Fast inference and lightweight reasoning
Status: Behavioral description — architect's direct experience
What it does:
Handles requests that need simple logical inference, basic analysis, or straightforward responses. The first reasoning node in the pipeline. If it can resolve the request adequately, the pipeline does not escalate further.
Input: User message, 3BM tags, 8BM bundle (if activated)
Output: Quick markdown — 1-3 sentence to short paragraph response draft
Activates: On fast route. Always activates when reasoning is needed.
8BR — Structured Reasoning
Model: 8B class
Role: Multi-step analysis and structured reasoning
Status: Behavioral description — architect's direct experience
What it does:
Handles requests that 3BR cannot resolve adequately — multi-step reasoning chains, structured analysis, complex explanations. Produces a more developed response than 3BR.
Input: User message, 3BM tags, 8BM bundle, 3BR quick output
Output: Deep markdown — 1 to 3 paragraphs, structured analysis format
Activates: On deep route, when complexity flags fire
14BR — Judicial Review
Model: 14B class
Role: Ethical evaluation and constitutional review
Status: Behavioral description — architect's direct experience
What it does:
The judicial node. Evaluates whether a proposed response or action is constitutionally permissible and ethically sound. Activated specifically for ethical questions and constitutional decisions — not for general complexity. Produces long-form analysis that the face node uses to deliver a constitutionally grounded response.
The artifact this node produces is called judicial_md in the pipeline — the name reflects its role as the constitutional review layer before final delivery.
Input: Full context chain — message, tags, bundle, quick reasoning, deep reasoning
Output: Judicial markdown — 3+ paragraphs, long-form constitutional analysis
Activates: When content flags fire — ethical questions, constitutional decisions, high-stakes governance requests
3BC, 8BC, 14BC — Code Nodes
Models: 3B, 8B, 14B class respectively
Role: Code explanation, generation, and system-level implementation
Status: Behavioral description — architect's direct experience
What they do:
Three-tier code handling following the same escalation pattern as reasoning nodes.
3BC — code explanation and simple script generation
8BC — single-file system generation, moderate complexity
14BC — multi-file architecture, system-level code generation
Code generation at the 14BC level requires gate engine approval — a Y/N confirmation before execution. This was enforced by the gate engine in the running system.
Gate requirement: 14BC and code_core steps require explicit user confirmation before proceeding. Auto-deny if gate engine cannot be reached.
9BF — Orchestration Face
Model: Gemma 9B
Role: Final response synthesis and delivery
Status: Behavioral description, confirmed by front_brain architecture
What it does:
The face of the system. Takes the full artifact chain produced by all activated nodes and synthesizes a coherent final response for the user. This is the node the user interacts with — all other nodes are invisible pipeline stages.
Also serves as the complex routing model for the planner when surface-level classification by 2.2B is insufficient.
Input: Full artifact chain — tags, bundle, quick, deep, judicial outputs
Output: Final user-facing response
Activates: Always. Every request ends at 9BF.
The Gate Engine
The gate engine enforces constitutional constraints on high-impact actions before they execute. It was a running component of the system, not a theoretical layer.
Gate-Required Actions
Long-term memory writes (LTM commits)
Code generation at depth (code_deep, code_core)
Any action flagged as high-risk by the planner
Gate Decisions
auto_approve — low risk, proceed
warn — high impact detected, present Y/N to user, proceed only on Y
auto_deny — constitutional violation or gate error, strip risky steps from route and proceed with safe subset
Gate Behavior on Error
If the gate engine cannot be reached, the decision defaults to auto_deny and risky steps are stripped. The system fails safe — it does less rather than proceeding without constitutional review.
